<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobo Árbenz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacobo Árbenz was a Guatemalan army officer and politician who was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1951 to 1954. A leader of the revolution of 1944, he carried out a land reform that expropriated uncultivated land of large estates, including that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>. Accused of communist sympathies, he was overthrown in 1954 by a coup organised by the American CIA, and died in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz.jpg" alt="Jacobo Árbenz" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobierno de Guatemala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Presidente_Jacobo_%C3%81rbenz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Jacobo Árbenz Guzmán was born on 14 September 1913 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a>, the son of a Swiss German pharmacist who had emigrated in 1901 and a Guatemalan schoolteacher. His father's morphine addiction ruined the family business, and since the family could not afford a university, Árbenz took a scholarship at the Polytechnic School as a military cadet in 1932. He graduated in 1935 and was the school's first sergeant, an honour given to only six cadets from 1924 to 1944. As a junior officer he had to lead squads escorting chain gangs of prisoners under the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a>, an experience that traumatised him and shaped his views.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ubico fell in 1944 after protests that included students, workers and junior officers, and a successor junta under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-ponce-vaides">Federico Ponce Vaides</a> took over. Árbenz, who had been living in El Salvador, was one of the leaders of the plot within the army, and insisted that civilians take part. After the October revolution he served as Minister of National Defense from 1944 to 1950 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo">Juan José Arévalo</a> and helped put down a military coup in 1949. He resigned on 20 February 1950 to run for the presidency, and he easily defeated Miguel Ydígoras Fuentes, taking office on 15 March 1951.</p><p>The centrepiece of his programme was the agrarian reform law, passed by the National Assembly on 17 June 1952, which transferred uncultivated land from large estates to poor labourers, with compensation. When he took office, 2% of the population owned 70% of the land. About 500,000 people benefited, most of them indigenous. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, the country's largest landowner and employer, lobbied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government against him, and Washington also feared communist influence. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> authorised the CIA's Operation PBSuccess in August 1953, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-castillo-armas">Carlos Castillo Armas</a> with about 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and the coup of 1954 ousted Árbenz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Árbenz and his family spent 73 days in the Mexican embassy before leaving the country, then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> from May 1957 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from July 1960. His daughter Arabella died by suicide in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in 1965, and he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where he died on 27 January 1971. In October 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s president Álvaro Colom apologised for the government's role in his overthrow. Historians dispute his legacy, which they say the CIA's media campaign shaped. Greg Grandin wrote that the land reform decree represented a fundamental shift in the power relations governing Guatemala, though the United States-backed military governments that followed reversed much of the reforms of Árbenz and Arévalo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacobo Árbenz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A young Argentine doctor, Ernesto Guevara, was living in Guatemala City when the CIA-backed coup toppled <b>Árbenz</b> in 1954, an experience that radicalised the future ‘Che’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.83,-91.52&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Quetzaltenango on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2048 1612 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacobo Árbenz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2498 1882h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2498 1882h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.83,-91.52&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
